{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nTeams plan and ship work by release in Jira, using the issue field `fixVersions`. rebar has no release concept, so the bridge drops that field:\n- The reconciler's field contract (`src/rebar/reconciler/_field_contract.py`) registers 11 fields: status, links, parent, title, assignee, priority, type, description, comments, labels and watchers. Nothing in `src/rebar` reads `fixVersions`.\n- Compiled ticket state (`src/rebar/reducer/_state.py`) has no release field.\n- No CLI, MCP or library surface can show, filter or set a release.\n\nSo an agent or person working from rebar can't answer \"what is in release X?\" or \"which release is this ticket targeted at?\" without going back to Jira. Release membership is also invisible to `next-batch` and `ready`, and to metrics.\n\n## Goal\n\nMake release a first-class rebar ticket field, synced in both directions with Jira's `fixVersions` through the existing reconciler. A fix-version change on either side appears on the other after the next reconcile pass, and rebar shows and filters by it in `rebar show`, `list` and the MCP read tools.\n\n## Approach\n\nFollow the shape of existing fields; don't add a parallel subsystem.\n\n1. **ADR first.** Record the design under `docs/adr/`. It must cover:\n   - the field's shape;\n   - the replayed event that carries it (prefer `EDIT.fields`, which replay already merges last-wins, over a new event type);\n   - its symmetry class in `FIELD_CONTRACT`;\n   - how Jira version identity maps to rebar (version `id` versus `name`, and renames);\n   - what happens when a Jira version is archived or deleted.\n2. **Store and reducer.** Add the field to compiled state with a default, and compact it into `SNAPSHOT`. Older stores replay unchanged, and older readers ignore the field under the schema forward-compatibility rules in `docs/event-schema.md`.\n3. **Reconciler inbound.**\n   - Fetch `fixVersions` for bridged issues in both Jira adapters (Cloud and Data Center, through the `jira_family` seam, ADR 0055).\n   - Translate the field in `inbound_translate.py` / `inbound_fields.py`, and diff it in `inbound_differ.py`.\n   - Apply it through `apply_inbound_events`.\n   - Register it in `FIELD_CONTRACT` and `conflict_resolver.FIELD_CLASSES`, so the contract property tests cover it.\n4. **Surfaces.**\n   - `rebar show`, the `list` / `search` filters, and the MCP read tools expose the field. Their `outputSchema`s and `docs/mcp-reference.md` are updated.\n   - The public-API surface census is updated deliberately.\n5. **Docs.** Update `docs/event-schema.md`, `docs/ticket-model.md`, `docs/jira-sync-setup.md`, and the reconciler record ADR 0121 where field coverage is enumerated.\n\n## List sync follows the tags/labels pattern (operator direction 2026-11-05)\n\nMirror each part of today's tags<->labels sync rather than inventing a new merge:\n- **Storage and replay:** tags live as a list in compiled state and change through per-event deltas. `TAG_DELTA {added, removed}` is applied by `rebar.reducer._processors.process_tag_delta`: removals first, then additions, so an add beats a remove in the same event and replay is deterministic. Releases get the same delta shape. The ADR decides whether that is a new delta event or a generalized one; no whole-list EDIT writer.\n- **Inbound (Jira->rebar):** `inbound_collection_diffs._diff_labels_inbound` set-diffs Jira labels against local tags. `apply_inbound_events._inbound_update_apply_labels` writes a TAG_DELTA, reading current state only to suppress no-ops, and skips the write if the reducer fails rather than assuming an empty set. Inbound-origin events carry `source=inbound`.\n- **Outbound (rebar->Jira):** `outbound_labels._diff_labels` emits a REMOVE only for values in the local ever-seen intent set from `local_label_intent`. That set scans CREATE, EDIT, TAG_DELTA and SNAPSHOT and skips inbound-origin events (bug a06c), so a value Jira added and rebar never had is never removed from Jira.\n- **Jira writes:** labels change through per-item update verbs (`{\"update\":{\"labels\":[{\"add\"|\"remove\": ...}]}}`, Data Center `add_field_value` / `update(remove)`), never a whole-list set, so a concurrent Jira-side edit is not overwritten. `fixVersions` is an array field and should take the same add/remove update verbs. This is unverified against the live API, because REB's edit screen omits the field; the first child checks it.\n- **Contract:** `FIELD_CONTRACT` `add_wins`, conflict class `set`, with the property tests covering it as they cover labels.\n\n## Design questions to settle in the ADR (each one changes the children's plans)\n\n- **Cardinality: DECIDED 2026-11-05 by the operator: a list of releases**, mirroring Jira's `fixVersions` (schema `{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":\"version\"}`, read from the live field API), with labels' add-wins rule: a release added on one side and removed on the other in the same pass stays added (`FIELD_CONTRACT` symmetry `add_wins`, conflict class `set`).\n- **Direction: DECIDED 2026-11-05 by the operator: both directions.** rebar can set the release, the bridge writes it to Jira `fixVersions`, and Jira changes sync into rebar. Both sides may edit; conflicts resolve like other bridged fields. The ADR must cover writing a version the Jira project does not have (Jira rejects unknown version names on update) and the bridge account's edit permission.\n- **Release as an entity.** Either store only the release name on the ticket, or also keep release records (Jira version id, name, released flag, release date) that rebar can list on their own.\n- **Affects versions.** Jira's separate `versions` field (affected versions) is out of scope unless the ADR brings it in.\n\n## Jira prerequisites found 2026-11-05\n\n- REB defines 0 versions, and no issue on the site has a fix version set.\n- REB's edit screen does not expose `fixVersions`: the REB-7015 editmeta omits it. So rebar→Jira writes need a Jira admin to add Fix versions to REB's edit screen, and the versions must exist in the project. The ADR states who creates versions.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- **Portability.** Works with any or no CI provider. A store with no Jira bridge has the field empty, and every command behaves as before.\n- **No config key.** A bridged project needs no new setting. The field syncs whenever the bridge runs. Any new mechanism needs a marker that names an open sunset ticket under this epic.\n- **Module size.** The 800-line cap, complexity ratchet and mechanism ratchet stay green. Split along existing seams; don't grow near-cap reconciler modules.\n- **No drift on old tickets.** Existing bridged tickets must not churn: a first pass over an unchanged store writes only the new field's initial values. A repeat pass writes nothing.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An ADR under `docs/adr/` records the field shape, event carrier, symmetry class, Jira version identity mapping, and archived/deleted version handling. Each design question above is answered there.\n- [ ] Compiled ticket state carries the release field, with an empty default. `SNAPSHOT` compaction preserves it, and replaying an existing store unchanged yields an empty value for every ticket. Pinned by reducer and compaction tests.\n- [ ] A reconcile pass that sees a bridged Jira issue's `fixVersions` change writes the matching rebar event, and a rebar-side release change updates the Jira issue's `fixVersions`. A repeat pass with no change on either side writes nothing. Pinned for both the Jira Cloud and Data Center adapters by tests over recorded API payloads.\n- [ ] The field is registered in `FIELD_CONTRACT` and `conflict_resolver.FIELD_CLASSES`, and the contract property tests pass for it.\n- [ ] `rebar show` displays the field, `rebar edit` (and the MCP/library edit surfaces) can set it, and `rebar list` can filter by release. The MCP read tools return it in their declared `outputSchema`. Pinned by CLI, MCP and library tests.\n- [ ] `docs/event-schema.md`, `docs/ticket-model.md`, `docs/jira-sync-setup.md` and `docs/mcp-reference.md` describe the field. The doc-drift tests pass.\n- [ ] A store with no Jira bridge passes the existing test suite unchanged.\n- [ ] `make verify` exits 0 on the final child's merge.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] On the production bridge, after deploy, one REB issue's fix version set in Jira appears on its bound rebar ticket after the next reconcile pass, and a release set on a rebar ticket appears on its Jira issue after the next pass. Shown with `rebar show` and the Jira issue view, and recorded in a ticket comment with the pass times."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793914664060331001,"uuid":"f10bf9ba-095a-4960-98b6-9f9799bc2d00"}